{#
  Full case study page, at /case-study/{slug} (the caseStudies structure section,
  routed via _routers/structure.twig).

  Built on the same `_components/content` shape as the contact single — whisper →
  title → prose on the cream slab, joined by the dotted spine — so a case study reads
  as part of the same family of static pages rather than a one-off layout.

  Field mapping:
    whisper  "Case Study" — a fixed label, not a field
    title    Long title, falling back to the study's own Title
    client   The related Client List entry (`clientSelector`), not a field on the study
    columns  Case Summary in the first column; the project facts in the second
    aside    "On this page" — the Case Content's h2s, where Enable Sidebar
             Summary is on (the note's switch, on this entry type too)
#}
{% extends '_layouts/page.twig' %}

{% set tplPath = _self %}

{# Cream slab, as on the contact page — the default is the olive p500. #}
{% set slabModifier = 'b-slab--l600' %}

{% block siteContent %}
    {# `longTitle ?: title` — the pattern every other section uses (see the contact
       single and the case study index). Both are the STUDY's own names now: Title is
       the short one it's filed under and the one the cards use, Long title the fuller
       sentence that only belongs on its own page. Neither is the client — a client can
       have several studies, so the client lives on the `clientSelector` relation and is
       read through craft.frontend, which every other surface reads too. #}
    {% set heading = (entry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: entry.title|trim %}

    {# The long form here — this row IS the client, so it gets the full name rather
       than the short one the card eyebrow uses. '' when no client is related, which
       drops the row entirely (project-meta filters empties): personal work has no
       client to name, and inventing one from the study's own title is what this
       change exists to stop. #}
    {% set client = craft.frontend.caseStudyClientName(entry, true) %}

    {# Case Summary — the CKEditor field, so it arrives as trusted HTML and goes into
       the column raw. NOT `summary`, which is the plain one-line description used for
       the cards and the model's background list. #}
    {% set caseSummary = entry.caseSummary ?? '' %}

    {# Project facts for the second column.

       Role is the Skills categories — the same source the Jonson background list
       uses, so what a study says it involved is stated once and read everywhere.
       Passed as a LIST rather than joined here, so project-meta can cap it: a study
       can carry a dozen skills, and the whole set dumped into the column buried the
       Year row below it. Six show, the rest sit behind a "+N more" toggle — all of
       them in the HTML either way (see project-meta.twig).

       Year comes from the entry's Post Date: no extra field to keep in step, and it's
       already set per entry. Set it to the year the project STARTED rather than the
       day the page was published. The "Is present?" switch makes it a range that's
       still open — "2015 – present" — for work that's ongoing. The "Date hidden?"
       switch drops the row entirely, for work old enough that dating it would
       undersell it. An empty value is all project-meta.twig needs to leave the row
       out. #}
    {% set role = entry.skills.all()|map(s => s.title)|values %}
    {% set year = (entry.hasDateHidden ?? false) or not entry.postDate
        ? ''
        : entry.postDate|date('Y') ~ ((entry.isPresent ?? false) ? ' – present' : '') %}

    {# The Sector Experience categories, the same source the Jonson background list
       reads as `sectors` (FindContext::categoryTitles). Passed as a LIST rather than
       joined here so project-meta renders them as separate items and the comma
       separators stay its business — and so a study that grows a long set can be
       capped later with `limit`, the way Role is. Uncapped for now: studies carry one
       to three of these, well short of the point where the row buries the ones below.
       A study with none simply loses the row; project-meta drops empties. #}
    {% set sectors = entry.sectorSelector.all()
        |map(c => (c.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: c.title)|values %}

    {# No Bio row. The field is the study's one-line public blurb and it still earns
       its keep everywhere it isn't visible on this page — the meta description and
       the JSON-LD description fall back to it (see page/head + page/scripts), and it
       is what Jonson reads as a study's summary (FindContext). It's just not a
       project FACT the way the rows below are, and it restated the standfirst the
       reader has already met in the column alongside. Add it back as a row here if
       that changes; don't empty the field. #}
    {% set meta = [
        { label: 'Client', value: client },
        { label: sectors|length > 1 ? 'Categories' : 'Category', values: sectors },
        { label: 'Role', values: role, limit: 6 },
        { label: 'Year', value: year },
    ] %}

    {# --case-study widens the h1 only (see _content.scss). A study's Long title is a
       full descriptive sentence rather than a page name, so it wraps to three lines
       at the shared title measure; the whisper and prose stay where they are. #}
    {# Column 1 sits under the h1 at the title's width rather than the narrower prose
       measure. The second column is empty for now — add its content as the second
       item in the list. #}
    {# No `ctas` for now — the ask bar below and the site footer both already offer a
       way to get in touch, and a third block of the same buttons mid-page was one too
       many. Pass `ctas` + `ctasHeading` again to bring them back. #}
    {# A fixed label rather than the client name. It says what the page IS, which is
       what the whisper does on every other view — and the client now has its own row
       in the meta column, so putting it up here as well would state it twice. #}
    {# `readMore` collapses the column to its opening paragraph on mount (read-more.js);
       the markup itself ships open, so it stays readable with JS off.
       (Set OUTSIDE the include below: a Twig comment inside a hash literal is a parse
       error, and the whole page 500s.) #}
    {% set col1 = caseSummary ? { html: caseSummary, readMore: true } : '' %}
    {% set col2 = include('_components/project-meta', { items: meta }, with_context = false) %}

    {# NO testimonial slider at the foot of the page, and no query behind one. A quote
       appears on a study only where a caseTestimonial block puts it, in the order the
       blocks are in — placement is the switch. This page used to also pull every
       testimonial related by caseStudyLink and slide them in below the work, which
       meant a quote nobody had placed still turned up, and the one you HAD placed
       turned up twice. The relation still earns its keep elsewhere (the index, and
       craft.frontend.testimonialCompany); it just no longer decides this page. #}

    {# Case Content, block by block. Rendered here rather than passed as entries, so
       _components/content stays generic — it takes finished HTML and knows nothing
       about matrix block types. The list is kept for the heading anchors below. #}
    {% set contentBlocks = entry.caseContent.all() %}

    {# The "on this page" panel — the same one a note carries (see
       _views/channel/notes): only where the study's Enable Sidebar Summary switch
       is on, and only with at least two h2s to list. The h2s get ids (the
       anchored HTML goes back into the blocks) and the list becomes the nav in
       the content's aside, beside the blocks. #}
    {% set anchors = (entry.enableSidebarSummary ?? false) ? craft.frontend.headingAnchors(contentBlocks) : null %}
    {# The STRIP, not the notes panel. Same switch, same anchors — only the surface
       changes, and it has to: the notes TOC is a column beside the prose, and a case
       study is mostly full-bleed imagery, so that column sits on the work. This one
       pins to the top of the window, appears once the reader is into the body, and
       keeps its list closed until it is asked for. See _components/case-toc.

       Rendered OUTSIDE the content component rather than into its aside, because it is
       fixed to the viewport and belongs to the page, not to the column layout. #}
    {% set caseToc = anchors and anchors.headings|length >= 2
        ? include('_components/case-toc', { headings: anchors.headings }, with_context = false)
        : null %}

    {# `anchoredHtml` is gated on the STRIP now, not on the old panel variable: it is
       what puts the generated ids into the block HTML, and without it the strip's
       anchors would point at headings that have no id.

       Said HERE, above the hash — a {# comment inside a hash or array literal is a Twig
       parse error and 500s the page. #}
    {% set anchoredHtml = caseToc ? anchors.html : {} %}
    {% set blocks = include('_components/case-content', {
        blocks: contentBlocks,
        anchoredHtml: anchoredHtml,
    }, with_context = false) %}

    {# The other work, so the page ends with somewhere to go rather than stopping
       dead at the last image.

       Same source and ordering as the index — live only, featured first — less this
       study: a strip offering the page you are already on reads as a broken link,
       and on a site with five studies losing one to a self-reference is a fifth of
       the strip. #}
    {% set others = craft.entries()
        .section('caseStudies')
        .status('live')
        .id('not ' ~ entry.id)
        .all() %}
    {% set others = others|filter(s => s.isFeatured ?? false)
        |merge(others|filter(s => not (s.isFeatured ?? false)))|values %}

    {# Then the other studies for THIS client to the front, each group keeping the
       featured ordering above. Someone at the foot of one study for a client is more
       likely to want that client's other work than a stranger's — and with several
       studies to a client, that work is otherwise buried at whatever position the
       featured sort left it. A study with no client, or a client with only this one,
       gets the list back untouched. #}
    {% set others = craft.frontend.caseStudiesBySharedClient(others, entry) %}

    {# asideOverlay: the "On this page" panel floats over the blocks rather than
       insetting them — a study's full-width imagery is the content, and must keep
       its width. (Said here, not in the hash: a comment inside the literal is a
       parse error that 500s the page.) #}
    {# The strip itself. Fixed to the viewport, so it sits at the top of the page's own
       content rather than inside the content component's column layout.

       Note there is no `aside` passed to _components/content below any more: the
       section nav is this strip, not a column beside the prose, which is the whole
       point — a column here sat on the imagery. #}
    {{ caseToc|raw }}

    {% include '_components/content' with {
        modifier: 'case-study',
        whisper: 'Case Study',
        title: heading,
        columns: [col1, col2],
        blocks: blocks,
        caseStudies: others,
        caseStudiesTitle: 'More case studies',
    } only %}

    {# Ask bar as a real footer — a SIBLING of .c-content, not a child, exactly as on
       the case study index. The timeline's end node is .c-content::after, so anything
       inside would sit above it; out here it also spans the slab without negative
       margins, since the inline padding belongs to .c-content. #}
    {# With a back button up to the case studies index, beside the home one. #}
    {% set studiesIndex = craft.entries().section('caseStudy').status('live').one() %}
    {% include '_components/ask' with {
        entry: entry,
        up: studiesIndex ? studiesIndex.url : siteUrl('case-studies'),
        upLabel: 'Back to case studies',
    } only %}
{% endblock %}
